# Record descriptors of the arv catalogue (GNU recutils), in the order the records are
# Compiled into arv (data.c) and used by the CLI and its JSON interface.
# Specification: docs/spec/smart-archive-format.md

%rec: Home
%doc: This archive's identity: one per home catalogue, made by arv init (or on first use).
+ Each home is its own privacy sphere: a disc carries the catalogue of its home only, and
+ names it (HomeUuid in catalog.rec). A home rebuilt from a disc keeps the disc's Uuid;
+ arv rebuild refuses a disc of another home unless told otherwise. Name is for people.
%key: Uuid
%mandatory: Uuid
%type: Uuid uuid
%type: Date date

%rec: Disc
%doc: One record per physical disc image (an OAIS AIP). Field names follow
+ Dublin Core terms where one fits: Title, Creator, Date, Description,
+ Subject, Coverage, Rights. Uuid is the machine identity of the image
+ (copies burned from one image share it); Id is for people and is
+ derived from Set, Sequence and Coverage (EDTF) under IdScheme, so it
+ can always be regenerated and checked (see docs/spec/smart-archive-format.md).
+ Layout, when given, is how its tree was made from its objects' tags: levels split by
+ /, alternatives by , (year/event, year); each object placed whole.
%key: Id
%mandatory: Id Title Date
%type: Uuid uuid
%type: Sequence int
%type: Date date
%type: Files int
%type: Bytes int
%type: Access enum public private sealed
%type: Retired date

%rec: Binding
%doc: How one volume is stored on its medium: the container (filesystem) and the
+ protection (error correction) it was made with. These change with the medium;
+ the files and the rest of the catalogue do not (research/plan.md, four layers).
+ Container: iso9660+udf-1.02, udf-2.50 (later perhaps ltfs, exfat, tar, afs).
+ Protection: rs03 or none. Media, Filesystem and Ecc describe them for people
+ (format 0.2 and earlier kept those three in the Disc record).
%key: Volume
%mandatory: Volume Container Protection
%type: MediumSectors int

%rec: Location
%doc: Places where copies are kept: site, room, shelf, box. Parent makes a tree,
+ so moving a box moves every copy in it. A copy event's Location field names a Code here,
+ or is free text. Temperature says how
+ copies kept there are used: hot (in active use: working files, a synced folder), warm
+ (online or reachable, but left alone: an .iso or archive folder on a NAS) or cold
+ (offline: discs on a shelf, unplugged drives); a place without one takes its parent's.
%key: Code
%mandatory: Code Name
%type: Parent rec Location


%rec: Collection
%doc: Something kept over time and made into discs again and again, e.g. a family's
+ photos: one workflow folder, one history of Revision records. Uuid is its identity
+ (it survives renames, moves and changes; the folder's .arv marker carries it); Code
+ is for people and prefixes its disc ids. Title, Description, Set, Category and
+ Access are the defaults its discs are made with.
%key: Code
%mandatory: Code Uuid Title
%type: Uuid uuid
%type: Access enum public private sealed

%rec: Revision
%doc: One edition of a collection: the state it was in when it became discs, appended, never
+ edited. Tree is the SHA-256 of its manifest (every file's path and SHA-256, sorted); Node is
+ the SHA-256 of Tree, Parent(s), Date and Message, and names the revision. Edition numbers
+ it, Volume names each disc. An edition is a set of discs made together,
+ each holding part of the collection and a copy of the catalogue, with as many
+ copies as its owner makes (discs, NAS); a later safe edition replaces earlier ones,
+ except those with Keep: yes (which may be added later: it is not part of Node).
%key: Node
%mandatory: Node Collection Tree Date Edition
%type: Date date
%type: Edition int
%type: Keep enum yes

%rec: Object
%doc: A data object: one file or folder put on a disc as a whole (a disc plan's item, or a
+ folder arv make puts on one disc), with no .arv marker of its own. Uuid is its lineage:
+ every version of the same object shares it.
+ Tree is the SHA-256 of its manifest (each file's SHA-256 and path within the object,
+ sorted; a repository's .git aside, as git.tsv describes it); Version counts its versions,
+ and the same Tree on another disc is the same version. Disc and Path say where it is
+ (Path under data/; "." for the whole of data/). Source is where it was read from: it is
+ kept in the home catalogue only, and discs carry their objects without it. Except (one
+ for each) names a file or folder of the source left out of the object because a disc plan
+ placed it elsewhere (a folder split): the object is the folder without them. Arranged, on a disc with
+ a Layout, is where it was arranged (its path in the disc plan) before the layout placed it
+ at Path; Name keeps its name where it was read from, and Slug, when they differ, is its name
+ on this disc. SameBytes (UUID VERSION) names an
+ object of another lineage with the same Tree: identity is the lineage, never the bytes,
+ so the same bytes read from elsewhere are another object, and this says so; a person who
+ knows they are one says so with an event (Object: object:THIS, SameAs: object:OTHER), and
+ readers then read its records as the other's versions with the same Tree.
+ Packed, it is one file on the disc: Pack names that file under data/ (the object's own, next to
+ its Path, or the disc's data/pack.*), and Packing says what it is (tar, tar+zstd, tar+xz,
+ tar+gzip, tar+lzip, tar+bzip2, zip; for a file also zstd, xz, gzip, lzip, bzip2). Unpacked where
+ it is, a pack puts each object it holds at its Path; Tree, Files and Bytes are those of its files.
+ Without Pack, Packing says the file is itself an archive (7z too), as it was given.
+ Title, Description, Coverage (EDTF), Rights and Origin (where it was published, as host/path)
+ say what it is: read from its own files when it is archived (its README, package manifest,
+ licence, a repository's commits and origin remote), or written by a person or a model
+ (arv describe NAME), with Notes.
%mandatory: Uuid Version Tree Disc Path Kind Date
%type: Uuid uuid
%type: Version int
%type: Files int
%type: Bytes int
%type: Date date
%type: Kind enum file folder git

%rec: Event
%doc: Preservation actions and metadata changes, appended and never edited. Type uses
+ the PREMIS eventType vocabulary: https://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/preservation/eventType
+ Disc names the disc; a change to a location or a data object names it in Object
+ (location:CODE, object:UUID; name:KEY for an entry of the known names, kept at home) instead.
+ Agent (one or more) is software (arv@COMMIT), a model (llm:MODEL, embeddings:MODEL) or a person (human:LOGIN).
+ Authorship says how people and machines shared the work: automatic (software
+ following rules), suggested (a model, not reviewed), accepted (a model's suggestion
+ a person kept as it was), edited (one a person changed), human (a person alone).
+ A replication records one copy: Copy (its letter, A, B ... unique among the disc's copies and
+ written on the disc after its id), Form (disc, iso: the image as a file, folder: the disc's
+ files), Temperature (hot, warm, cold: given, else its place's, else cold for a disc and
+ warm for a file or folder), Location (where this copy is kept), Path for a file or folder,
+ MediaType (the media's maker and type, as the drive reports it) and Identifier when known:
+ how this physical copy is recognised, as scheme:value (bca: its factory serial, barcode: a
+ label stuck on and scanned, serial: a manufacturer's serial), repeatable, and added later by
+ any event that names the Copy. With ReadBack: identical
+ it is a copy read back against its image before it was recorded (known good), as is one a
+ later fixity check with its Copy passed. A disc's copies are its replications, counted as
+ physical discs: two that share a bca:, barcode: or serial: identifier are one disc recorded
+ twice. A stored copy may name its Carrier, the tape or drive holding it (and other discs'
+ copies): two copies of one disc on one carrier count as one.
+ Every event has an EventId: the first 32 hex digits of the SHA-256 of its other fields as
+ written (a Nonce keeps two alike apart). An event about one copy names it by Replication (that
+ copy's replication EventId) and Copy (its letter then; the newest is its letter now). A change
+ to a field says Sets: FIELD and Replaces: the EventIds of the changes it follows (a path's
+ tags or caption after burning: Tags: PATH<TAB>TAGS or Caption: PATH<TAB>TEXT, with Sets:
+ Tags:PATH or Caption:PATH; the disc's tags.tsv stays as burned; a data object's tags or
+ name now: an event about it, Object: object:UUID, with Tags: TAGS or Slug: NAME, Sets: Tags
+ or Slug); two that
+ replace the same one were made apart, a conflict to settle by setting the field again. Deaccession retires a disc (its Disc record gets
+ Retired).
%mandatory: Type Date Outcome
%type: Outcome enum success failure warning
%type: Authorship enum automatic suggested accepted edited human
%type: Disc rec Disc
%type: Location rec Location

%rec: Appraisal
%doc: The archivist log: how much something matters, to whom, and why. Appended and
+ never edited; the newest appraisal of a target replaces earlier ones, and one a
+ person made or reviewed outranks a machine's. Target is DISC-ID, DISC-ID:folder/,
+ DISC-ID:folder/file (relative to data/) or set:CODE; a target without an appraisal
+ takes the nearest one above it (file, folder, disc, set).
+ Importance is '<level> for <audience>', one per audience; levels, most first:
+ essential (must survive: 3 copies in 3 places), important (on disc: 2 in 2),
+ useful (on disc if there is room), incidental (everyday storage is enough).
+ Only a person can mark something incidental. Basis says why; Review is when to
+ look again. Authorship and Agent as in Event.
%mandatory: Target Date Authorship
%type: Date date
%type: Review date
%type: Authorship enum automatic suggested accepted edited human

%rec: Archive
%doc: Entry point of a smart-archive disc: which format and version this is, which disc,
+ and where its other catalogue files are (paths relative to the disc root).
+ Specification: tools/arv/docs/spec/smart-archive-format.md
%mandatory: Format Version Disc Uuid

%rec: Snapshot
%doc: When this catalogue snapshot was taken and what it covers. The home
+ archive.rec stays authoritative for anything recorded after this date.
